#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""lighttpdwho - which lighttpd condition block sets KEY for this request,
and at which file:line?

A static model of lighttpd's documented config-selection rules
(DESIGN-lighttpdwho.md, BUILD APPROVED 2026-11-09). Semantics pinned to
Docs_Configuration (lighttpd redmine, current), lighttpd(8), and the
2022-12 maintainer note on include resolution. Claims are tagged
[documented], [docs-inferred], or [hypothesized]; no lighttpd binary
exists on this box, so no claim here is [observed] and there is no
live-agreement test to skip-guard against: `lighttpd -p` prints a
flattened, provenance-free parsed form whose production requires
executing the config's own `include_shell` fragments, so it cannot
serve as a safe oracle. Hermetic fixture trees are the only oracle,
exactly as the manual text is the only authority.

Pinned semantics (LT01-LT27 in the design doc):
- LT01 per-key config selection, never request handling; a key with no
  applicable assignment is NOT-SET, never a guessed default.
- LT02 include chain: textual in-place insertion, in the enclosing
  scope; missing literal include and include cycles are fatal (exit 2).
- LT03 relative includes resolve against the MAIN config file's
  directory, not the including file's.
- LT04 include globs expand in byte-wise sorted order; a glob matching
  nothing is a warning, and a NOT-SET answer over an empty glob lists
  it as [possibly-missing].
- LT05 include_shell is NEVER executed; it is inventoried verbatim and
  poisons positionally (Kleene): absorbed by a later TRUE decider,
  otherwise the verdict is UNKNOWN with the shell line as blocker.
- LT06 global { } lifts assignments to root scope, at their stream
  position among root assignments.
- LT07 var./env. are parse-time and sequential; var.PID / var.CWD are
  daemon-runtime facts rendered as <PID-unknown> / <CWD-unknown>;
  env. comes only from --env, never from this process's environment.
- LT08 assignment operators are scope-local: = assigns, += merges onto
  the same scope's earlier value, := replaces.
- LT09 last matching condition wins; NOTHING merges across scopes.
  A conditional assignment replaces the root value whole.
- LT10 conditional fields/operators/version gates; legacy header aliases
  normalize to $REQUEST_HEADER[...] with a banner.
- LT11 no AND/OR operators; a header with && / || is unparseable.
- LT12 nesting is AND; the nested scope is later in precedence.
- LT13 else/elif chains short-circuit; a branch matches iff all previous
  branches are FALSE and its own test is TRUE; a previous UNKNOWN makes
  later branches UNKNOWN; else attaches only to the immediately
  preceding block in the same file.
- LT14 identical conditions at one level are merged at parse time.
- LT15 precedence: root is the base layer, then matching scopes in
  depth-first document order; a root line after a conditional never
  shadows it for a matching request.
- LT16 $HTTP["host"] is the authority as given; ==/!=/=^/=$ are
  case-insensitive, regexes run on the lowercased host; a host regex
  anchored at the end without a port allowance raises the
  [host-regex-port-trap] finding.
- LT17 $HTTP["remoteip"] is the peer address; CIDR only for ==/!=.
- LT18 url/querystring/scheme/method/headers; an absent header is the
  empty string; only a query field the operator did not supply is
  UNKNOWN -- nothing is ever defaulted.
- LT19 regexes are PCRE; Python `re` is a subset, never an
  approximation: unsupported constructs are [regex-unknown] (UNKNOWN),
  invalid patterns poison their scope.
- LT20 $SERVER["socket"] is evaluated against --socket as written, with
  omitted-IP expansion; a socket scope with no --socket is UNKNOWN.
- LT21 three-valued (Kleene) evaluation; the last TRUE-scope assignment
  is the decider unless an UNKNOWN-scope assignment sits after it.
- LT22 verdict vocabulary: EFFECTIVE (exit 0), NOT-SET (exit 1),
  UNKNOWN (exit 1).
- LT23 unparseable lines poison the selected answer positionally; one
  broken vhost must not blind the tool to the other forty.
- LT24 values are opaque, rendered as selected; %0-%9 / $0-$9 /
  ${url.*} tokens are rendered verbatim, never evaluated.
- LT25 runtime vhost modules (mod_simple_vhost, mod_evhost,
  mod_vhostdb, mod_mysql_vhost, mod_magnet) make server.document-root
  UNKNOWN; for other keys their presence is a banner only.
- LT26 safety: pure text parsing; no subprocess, no network, no writes,
  no environment reads. The only value printed is the queried key's.
- LT27 coverage on every run: files read, includes, scopes, keys,
  gated assignments, dependencies, unparseable lines.

The tool never runs lighttpd, never executes anything in the config,
and never reads the process environment.
"""

